00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
to say that the environment secretary  george eustis uh joins us on the program  mr eustis great to have you on and uh  thank you so much for for braving the  cold i know exactly how cold it is uh  just outside the office this morning so  let's get rattling on as you just heard  we're we're running a series of special  programs at this juncture in our  response to covid19 to to assess where  where things have gone wrong and perhaps  some of the lessons learned i wonder if  you might identify one area in which we  we have as a as a country changed our  response  one mistake that we made and have  learned from  well look it's been an emerging  situation and evidence has developed as  time has gone on and of course we've  changed our approach in some areas so  initially the advice was that uh there  wasn't a great deal of value in wearing  face masks  as the evidence emerged the world health  organization changed that view and we  changed  our advice in line with that so  yes this has been a dynamic evolving  situation the government's you know  responded to that and changed its  position in some areas for instance face  um  masks  i wonder then if we might dwell just  briefly it is away from your brief but  on on universal credit then there is a  real pressure um from recipients of the  the 20-pound uplift to politicians on  your side of the the chamber of the  house of commons  is there any possibility that you know  in the immediate future we might get a  decision as to whether or not that that  20 pound uplift might last substantially  longer perhaps as long as a year  well look obviously it's got a few more  months uh to run i think a couple more  months to run and um rishi sunak i'm  sure when he does his budget we'll be  looking at all these issues carefully we  do know that uh you know it's affected  household incomes and the resilience of  households this uh lockdown and the  pandemic generally we know and we put in  place measures to help some of the most  vulnerable afford food because there's  been some issues with families unable to  afford food we've put new measures in to  support them and we've had this  temporary uplift in the universal credit  at some point we need to get back to  normal get people back uh working again  earning full salaries again that's got  to be  the route to recovery but i'm sure that  uh as we emerge from the lockdown you  know rishi sunak will be looking  carefully at the types of measures that  will be needed uh to make that  transition from lockdown back to life as  normal  um well let's talk about the variants  that are currently in the country at the  moment and clearly there has been a  degree of concern about about the south  african variant particularly because of  fears around the efficacy of various uh  vaccines i i wonder if you might kind of  reiterate the points made by jonathan  van tam at yesterday's news conference  that look you know we have to be mindful  we have to be concerned about these new  variants but the one which we are  dealing with in the united kingdom well  the vaccines do pretty well with that  yes and that remains uh our main  challenge the moment is dealing with a  so-called kent variant which is uh  highly infectious spreads more quickly  than some of the other variants  and dominates at the moment and is our  primary challenge and the vaccines are  fully efficacious against that  particular strain but we constantly  monitor other variants around the world  there is always a risk that there will  be a variant that manages to to  circumvent uh the vaccines that we have  and that's why we have to constantly  redouble our efforts to evolve the  vaccine so that they can deal with any  new variants that might arrive but also  why we are you know constantly looking  and toughening our approach to  quarantine for those arriving from  overseas because until we have an  ability to to vary the vaccine and  update the vaccine to meet those new  challenges we do have to exercise some  caution about international travel and  that's why we put in place the measures  we have  sure but just in terms of the south  african variant  the case is numbered less than a couple  of hundred at the moment according to to  jonathan van tan but but given that  there is an eight-day process for us to  be able to sequence uh sequence an  individual case and discover whether  it's the south african uh variant given  the fact that we understand that  community transmission is taken has has  taken place  it is by no means beyond possibility  that we could see a surge in the south  african variant isn't it  well that we we can't be complacent but  i think what professor van tam said  yesterday is that the evidence so far  suggests that the south african variant  is actually less contagious than the  kent one so it travels at a at a lower  speed it hasn't got that uh that highly  uh infectious rate that we see in the  kent uh variant it's here in very small  numbers now so we have to obviously  monitor it very closely but it's also  the case that while there had been  concerns uh that some of the vaccines  yesterday vaccine might not be fully  effective against the south african  variant you know those concerns have  also receded somewhat in that you know  the the evidence seems to say that it it  prevents some of the most uh serious um  symptoms of the virus and therefore does  provide some protection  um once again minister i appear to be  learning from the papers that we're  going to be seeing some toughening up  around around our borders and you you  alluded to it a second ago is it the  case that we are now going to see  people who arrive here in the united  kingdom mandatory testing day two day  after their arrival  well look i i don't think a final  decision has been made on that but we do  keep all of this under review we've  stopped short of having a sort of total  closure of the border at this stage  we actually think that  it causes a lot of disruption but  doesn't add a huge amount whereas the  measures we have put in place already we  require a pre-travel test  and the carriers are required to check  that test before passengers board we  require everybody to quarantine for a  period when they arrive here and as you  know we've introduced new measures to  ensure that those coming from high-risk  countries will quarantine in hotels in  future there is a there is a case for  doing some  testing during quarantine we already  have a provision to so that they can  test to release from quarantine earlier  and we're obviously looking at this to  see whether we can strengthen these  measures further  if a decision has not yet been made why  once again am i learning about something  in the newspapers why once again has  briefing been made presumably by someone  relatively senior in the civil service  or relatively senior in government  and the papers are once again  speculating about it  well look i as you uh as you know neil i  was once a press secretary and you we  often end up reading things in the  papers that we would prefer not to um  that the reason i can only uh presume  that it has appeared in the papers is  that it is a subject that's being  discussed at the moment within  government i don't think a final  decision has been made but i understand  um you know matt hancock may be looking  at this further today  and obviously if a decision is made then  he'll make an announcement at that point  but um you know for now we've got in  place the measures we have we're  constantly looking at refining things we  don't rule out uh things like um  post quarantine or during quarantine  testing we already have it for people to  release from quarantine early but you  know i don't want to pre-judge anything  matt hancock might say  sure but but on another and particularly  related topic quarantine hotels we did  have the announcement that those would  be introduced at some point we had an  announcement that there would be an  announcement we still do not yet know  when that announcement is going to be  what we do learn from the papers this  morning is that perhaps one of the  reasons why we haven't been able to move  forward with that is that the government  hasn't been able to book a hotel room  well look i know that the department for  health are working on this working with  some of the hotel operators they're in  discussion about  procuring those hotel rooms  we've got a policy in place that from  the 15th of february that's next monday  people coming from those high-risk  countries we'll have to quarantine in  hotels i'm confident that we will have  that uh provision in place and i'm aware  that discussions are ongoing at the  moment  have we booked any hotels though because  the the strong implication in what  indeed the story in the papers this  morning is that we haven't booked them  we haven't been to any  well my understanding is and this is  obviously a procurement issue that  officials in the department of health  are dealing with my understanding is  that they are in discussion with  operators uh those discussions have not  yet concluded but they're confident that  they will conclude in good time for the  quarantine policy to start on the 15th  of february as planned  can i just talk briefly about vaccine  passports we heard from from the deans  of hawaii that vaccine passports not  something that the government is going  to be doing i just wonder what the  government's position is on companies  private publicly traded companies  insisting on people who access their  services demonstrating that they have  had a vaccine let's say restaurants  let's say pubs what would be the  government's position on that  well i think it'll hopefully be  unnecessary in the sense that  within the next week or so we hope to  have vaccinated all uh of the uh most  vulnerable cohorts all of the over 70s  were already um well on the way there  with i think over 90 percent of the over  75s now vaccinated and the reality is  that as we start to you know emerge from  lockdown when that moment comes and the  prime minister will be outlining that uh  that journey on the 22nd of february by  the time we start to loosen that lock  down then we we will have vaccinated all  of those most vulnerable cohorts and  therefore you know the risk recedes and  it probably wouldn't be worth a while of  any individual business trying to check  whether people have been vaccinated so  our approach to vaccination is to  vaccinate the most vulnerable cohorts in  order and to to roll out the vaccine as  quickly as possible i very very much  doubt that it'll be worth anybody's  while checking whether someone's been  vaccinated simply because i say many of  the most vulnerable will have been by  the time we get to easing lockdown  sure what what then of of within the  health services  itself the royal college of nursing has  released data that says that you know 15  of nurses remain unvaccinated and that  is for for a variety of reasons uh but  of those who remain unvaccinated sorry  of those nurses that they pulled four  percent had decided not to take the  vaccine can we conceive of circumstances  in which we would not insist on  frontline healthcare professionals  having the vaccine if they had been  offered it  well look i think it's always very  difficult to  require or mandate vaccination in that  way  it's always been the case that uh people  have to choose to to want it obviously  it's disappointing if there's people in  uh you know working in our nhs who  themselves in small very small numbers  have decided not to have the vaccine  we want to get maximum protection  particularly for those vulnerable  cohorts but also for those such as those  working in the nhs who are particularly  exposed and the vast majority of them  have taken up the vaccine

